1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,005
Sweetheart, you know what you asked me

2
00:00:03,005 --> 00:00:03,796
at dinner?

3
00:00:03,976 --> 00:00:07,384
You asked if your great-grandmother Dorothy had

4
00:00:07,384 --> 00:00:07,819
a job.

5
00:00:07,999 --> 00:00:09,288
And I laughed.

6
00:00:09,468 --> 00:00:12,096
Because she worked harder than anybody I

7
00:00:12,096 --> 00:00:13,148
have ever known.

8
00:00:13,328 --> 00:00:15,110
Fourteen hours a day,

9
00:00:15,110 --> 00:00:16,638
seven days a week,

10
00:00:16,638 --> 00:00:19,609
and nobody ever paid her one penny.

11
00:00:19,789 --> 00:00:22,180
She was a housewife in the nineteen

12
00:00:22,180 --> 00:00:22,726
fifties.

13
00:00:22,906 --> 00:00:24,963
And some of the things she had

14
00:00:24,963 --> 00:00:25,786
to do, well.

15
00:00:25,966 --> 00:00:28,020
You'd call a lawyer today.

16
00:00:28,371 --> 00:00:31,206
I'm not saying this to complain about

17
00:00:31,206 --> 00:00:32,202
the old days.

18
00:00:32,382 --> 00:00:35,225
I'm saying it because nobody wrote it

19
00:00:35,225 --> 00:00:35,609
down.

20
00:00:35,789 --> 00:00:37,803
They wrote down the wars and the

21
00:00:37,803 --> 00:00:38,495
presidents.

22
00:00:38,675 --> 00:00:41,288
Nobody wrote down what my mother did

23
00:00:41,288 --> 00:00:43,756
between six in the morning and ten

24
00:00:43,756 --> 00:00:44,410
at night.

25
00:00:44,590 --> 00:00:46,355
So I'm going to tell you.

26
00:00:46,535 --> 00:00:47,788
Count them with me.

27
00:00:48,139 --> 00:00:49,137
Number one.

28
00:00:49,317 --> 00:00:50,339
The day.

29
00:00:50,519 --> 00:00:53,214
My mother got up at five-thirty,

30
00:00:53,214 --> 00:00:54,478
before anybody.

31
00:00:54,658 --> 00:00:56,736
She put the coffee on in the

32
00:00:56,736 --> 00:00:59,036
percolator, she made breakfast,

33
00:00:59,036 --> 00:01:01,188
she packed three lunch pails,

34
00:01:01,188 --> 00:01:03,860
and she ironed my father's shirt for

35
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:06,903
that day, because you ironed every shirt,

36
00:01:06,903 --> 00:01:07,719
sweetheart.

37
00:01:07,899 --> 00:01:10,212
Every shirt, every sheet,

38
00:01:10,212 --> 00:01:14,006
every pillowcase, even the handkerchiefs.

39
00:01:14,186 --> 00:01:16,450
Tuesday was ironing day.

40
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:18,220
It took the whole day.

41
00:01:18,571 --> 00:01:20,544
Monday was wash day.

42
00:01:20,724 --> 00:01:22,671
We had a wringer washer in the

43
00:01:22,671 --> 00:01:23,255
basement.

44
00:01:23,435 --> 00:01:25,196
You filled it with a hose,

45
00:01:25,196 --> 00:01:27,837
you ran the clothes through two rollers

46
00:01:27,837 --> 00:01:29,530
to squeeze the water out,

47
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:32,104
and if you weren't careful the rollers

48
00:01:32,104 --> 00:01:33,594
took your fingers too.

49
00:01:33,774 --> 00:01:35,885
Mother had a scar on her hand

50
00:01:35,885 --> 00:01:36,467
from it.

51
00:01:36,647 --> 00:01:38,823
Then you carried the wet basket up

52
00:01:38,823 --> 00:01:41,190
the stairs and hung everything on the

53
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:42,278
line in the yard.

54
00:01:42,458 --> 00:01:44,223
Even in January.

55
00:01:44,403 --> 00:01:46,992
The sheets froze stiff as boards.

56
00:01:47,342 --> 00:01:48,754
And the whole time,

57
00:01:48,754 --> 00:01:50,984
the house had to be perfect by

58
00:01:50,984 --> 00:01:51,951
five o'clock.

59
00:01:52,131 --> 00:01:54,050
Because at five-thirty,

60
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:55,718
my father came home.

61
00:01:55,898 --> 00:01:57,454
And the magazines said,

62
00:01:57,454 --> 00:01:59,280
and I'm not making this up,

63
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,648
that a good wife should freshen her

64
00:02:01,648 --> 00:02:03,880
makeup, put a ribbon in her hair,

65
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:06,045
and have dinner hot on the table

66
00:02:06,045 --> 00:02:08,750
when her husband walks through the door.

67
00:02:08,930 --> 00:02:11,909
And never, ever complain to him about

68
00:02:11,909 --> 00:02:12,553
her day.

69
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:14,695
His day was more important.

70
00:02:15,045 --> 00:02:16,217
Number two.

71
00:02:16,397 --> 00:02:17,036
The money.

72
00:02:17,216 --> 00:02:19,399
My mother had no money of her

73
00:02:19,399 --> 00:02:19,700
own.

74
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:20,728
None.

75
00:02:20,908 --> 00:02:23,412
Every Friday my father gave her an

76
00:02:23,412 --> 00:02:25,180
allowance for the house.

77
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:26,626
Thirty dollars.

78
00:02:26,806 --> 00:02:29,320
Out of that came the groceries,

79
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:31,185
the milkman, our shoes,

80
00:02:31,185 --> 00:02:32,564
the church plate.

81
00:02:32,744 --> 00:02:35,398
If she wanted something for herself,

82
00:02:35,398 --> 00:02:37,168
a new pair of stockings,

83
00:02:37,168 --> 00:02:39,158
she had to save it from the

84
00:02:39,158 --> 00:02:41,591
grocery money a nickel at a time.

85
00:02:41,771 --> 00:02:43,652
She kept it in a coffee can

86
00:02:43,652 --> 00:02:44,836
behind the flour.

87
00:02:45,186 --> 00:02:47,786
And here's the part that shocks people.

88
00:02:47,966 --> 00:02:50,750
A married woman in those days couldn't

89
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:52,801
get a credit card in her own

90
00:02:52,801 --> 00:02:53,167
name.

91
00:02:53,347 --> 00:02:55,240
Not in most places.

92
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:58,044
The bank wanted her husband to sign.

93
00:02:58,224 --> 00:03:00,163
She couldn't take out a loan for

94
00:03:00,163 --> 00:03:01,254
a car without him.

95
00:03:01,434 --> 00:03:03,921
That didn't change until a law in

96
00:03:03,921 --> 00:03:05,579
nineteen seventy-four.

97
00:03:05,759 --> 00:03:08,628
My mother was fifty-three years old before

98
00:03:08,628 --> 00:03:10,678
she had a charge card with her

99
00:03:10,678 --> 00:03:11,703
own name on it.

100
00:03:11,883 --> 00:03:13,950
She framed the first bill.

101
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:16,355
And if she had wanted a job?

102
00:03:16,535 --> 00:03:17,417
Well.

103
00:03:17,597 --> 00:03:20,719
Many schools fired women teachers when they

104
00:03:20,719 --> 00:03:21,591
got married.

105
00:03:21,771 --> 00:03:25,540
Airlines fired stewardesses when they got married,

106
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:27,123
or turned thirty-two.

107
00:03:27,303 --> 00:03:29,094
My mother had wanted to be a

108
00:03:29,094 --> 00:03:30,565
teacher her whole life.

109
00:03:30,745 --> 00:03:32,647
She gave it up the week she

110
00:03:32,647 --> 00:03:33,915
married my father.

111
00:03:34,095 --> 00:03:36,626
Nobody asked her if she minded.

112
00:03:36,806 --> 00:03:38,327
Nobody thought to.

113
00:03:38,677 --> 00:03:39,977
Number three.

114
00:03:40,157 --> 00:03:41,109
The doctor.

115
00:03:41,289 --> 00:03:43,989
When my mother was expecting my brother

116
00:03:43,989 --> 00:03:46,343
Danny, the doctor told her to keep

117
00:03:46,343 --> 00:03:49,182
smoking, because it would calm her nerves

118
00:03:49,182 --> 00:03:50,844
and keep the baby small.

119
00:03:51,024 --> 00:03:52,575
Keep the baby small,

120
00:03:52,575 --> 00:03:54,669
like that was a good thing.

121
00:03:54,849 --> 00:03:56,945
She smoked a pack a day the

122
00:03:56,945 --> 00:03:57,798
whole time.

123
00:03:57,978 --> 00:04:00,219
Every mother on our street did.

124
00:04:00,569 --> 00:04:02,393
And when the baby came,

125
00:04:02,393 --> 00:04:05,723
sweetheart, they put the mothers to sleep.

126
00:04:05,903 --> 00:04:08,376
It was called twilight sleep.

127
00:04:08,556 --> 00:04:10,017
They gave you a shot,

128
00:04:10,017 --> 00:04:12,242
and you woke up hours later with

129
00:04:12,242 --> 00:04:14,676
a baby, and you didn't remember any

130
00:04:14,676 --> 00:04:15,093
of it.

131
00:04:15,273 --> 00:04:17,560
Fathers weren't allowed in the room.

132
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:19,959
They waited down the hall with a

133
00:04:19,959 --> 00:04:20,375
cigar.

134
00:04:20,555 --> 00:04:22,950
And then the mother stayed in the

135
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:24,837
hospital for a whole week,

136
00:04:24,837 --> 00:04:26,070
flat on her back,

137
00:04:26,070 --> 00:04:28,465
and the nurses kept the babies in

138
00:04:28,465 --> 00:04:30,134
a nursery behind glass.

139
00:04:30,484 --> 00:04:32,535
And when she was tired,

140
00:04:32,535 --> 00:04:33,784
honey, or sad,

141
00:04:33,784 --> 00:04:37,531
which happened, the doctor gave her pills.

142
00:04:37,711 --> 00:04:40,783
Little yellow pills called Miltown,

143
00:04:40,783 --> 00:04:42,099
a tranquilizer.

144
00:04:42,279 --> 00:04:45,919
In nineteen fifty-six it was the best-selling

145
00:04:45,919 --> 00:04:47,214
drug in America.

146
00:04:47,394 --> 00:04:49,571
Then came the diet pills,

147
00:04:49,571 --> 00:04:51,051
which were speed.

148
00:04:51,231 --> 00:04:53,431
Half the mothers on Elm Street were

149
00:04:53,431 --> 00:04:55,506
taking one in the morning and one

150
00:04:55,506 --> 00:04:56,072
at night.

151
00:04:56,252 --> 00:04:58,760
They called it mother's little helper.

152
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:01,088
Later there was a song about it.

153
00:05:01,438 --> 00:05:02,564
Number four.

154
00:05:02,744 --> 00:05:03,673
The kitchen.

155
00:05:03,853 --> 00:05:05,304
Now, I'll be fair,

156
00:05:05,304 --> 00:05:08,369
the kitchen got easier in the fifties.

157
00:05:08,549 --> 00:05:11,900
In nineteen fifty-three a company called Swanson

158
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:13,855
came out with the TV dinner.

159
00:05:14,035 --> 00:05:16,206
Turkey, cornbread dressing,

160
00:05:16,206 --> 00:05:18,135
peas and sweet potatoes,

161
00:05:18,135 --> 00:05:19,743
in an aluminum tray,

162
00:05:19,743 --> 00:05:21,593
for ninety-eight cents.

163
00:05:21,773 --> 00:05:24,318
The first year they sold ten million

164
00:05:24,318 --> 00:05:24,884
of them.

165
00:05:25,064 --> 00:05:27,398
My father refused to eat one.

166
00:05:27,578 --> 00:05:29,827
He said a man deserves a cooked

167
00:05:29,827 --> 00:05:30,190
meal.

168
00:05:30,370 --> 00:05:31,612
So Mother cooked.

169
00:05:31,963 --> 00:05:33,132
And the food,

170
00:05:33,132 --> 00:05:34,122
sweetheart.

171
00:05:34,302 --> 00:05:36,729
You would not believe the food.

172
00:05:36,909 --> 00:05:39,080
Everything went into Jell-O.

173
00:05:39,260 --> 00:05:41,907
Lime Jell-O with shredded carrots.

174
00:05:42,087 --> 00:05:45,477
Lemon Jell-O with tuna fish and olives.

175
00:05:45,657 --> 00:05:48,472
A ring of tomato aspic with shrimp

176
00:05:48,472 --> 00:05:51,288
inside it, like a little aquarium.

177
00:05:51,468 --> 00:05:53,942
Mother made a molded salad for every

178
00:05:53,942 --> 00:05:54,904
church supper.

179
00:05:55,084 --> 00:05:57,255
It was considered elegant.

180
00:05:57,435 --> 00:05:59,230
I would not feed it to a

181
00:05:59,230 --> 00:05:59,978
dog today.

182
00:06:00,328 --> 00:06:02,150
We had no dishwasher.

183
00:06:02,330 --> 00:06:04,345
Mother washed every dish,

184
00:06:04,345 --> 00:06:06,683
every pot, three times a day,

185
00:06:06,683 --> 00:06:08,376
by hand, in the sink,

186
00:06:08,376 --> 00:06:10,310
with a bar of soap and a

187
00:06:10,310 --> 00:06:10,794
cloth.

188
00:06:10,974 --> 00:06:12,147
I dried.

189
00:06:12,327 --> 00:06:15,181
Danny was supposed to put them away,

190
00:06:15,181 --> 00:06:16,529
and he never did.

191
00:06:16,709 --> 00:06:18,983
And the kitchen floor was linoleum,

192
00:06:18,983 --> 00:06:20,867
and she waxed it on her knees

193
00:06:20,867 --> 00:06:21,841
every Saturday.

194
00:06:22,021 --> 00:06:23,461
Every Saturday.

195
00:06:23,641 --> 00:06:26,125
My knees hurt just remembering it.

196
00:06:26,476 --> 00:06:27,834
Number five.

197
00:06:28,014 --> 00:06:30,139
The rules for how a lady looked.

198
00:06:30,319 --> 00:06:33,307
My mother never went downtown without a

199
00:06:33,307 --> 00:06:34,916
hat and white gloves.

200
00:06:35,096 --> 00:06:35,862
Never.

201
00:06:36,042 --> 00:06:37,226
Not to the bank,

202
00:06:37,226 --> 00:06:38,631
not to the dentist.

203
00:06:38,811 --> 00:06:41,579
She wore a girdle every single day

204
00:06:41,579 --> 00:06:42,556
of her life,

205
00:06:42,556 --> 00:06:43,777
even in August,

206
00:06:43,777 --> 00:06:45,812
even to hang the laundry.

207
00:06:45,992 --> 00:06:48,591
Rubber girdles, sweetheart,

208
00:06:48,591 --> 00:06:50,323
in Ohio in August.

209
00:06:50,503 --> 00:06:52,653
And stockings with a seam up the

210
00:06:52,653 --> 00:06:55,274
back that had to be perfectly straight.

211
00:06:55,625 --> 00:06:58,413
And she could never wear trousers to

212
00:06:58,413 --> 00:06:59,885
church, or to town.

213
00:07:00,065 --> 00:07:02,742
A woman in pants downtown was talked

214
00:07:02,742 --> 00:07:03,188
about.

215
00:07:03,368 --> 00:07:06,140
Mother wore pedal pushers in the garden,

216
00:07:06,140 --> 00:07:08,357
and if the doorbell rang she ran

217
00:07:08,357 --> 00:07:10,713
upstairs and put on a skirt before

218
00:07:10,713 --> 00:07:12,099
she opened the door.

219
00:07:12,279 --> 00:07:13,580
I'm serious.

220
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:15,676
I watched her do it for the

221
00:07:15,676 --> 00:07:16,883
Fuller Brush man.

222
00:07:17,233 --> 00:07:19,343
Every August, sweetheart,

223
00:07:19,343 --> 00:07:22,213
our kitchen turned into a factory.

224
00:07:22,393 --> 00:07:23,647
Canning season.

225
00:07:23,827 --> 00:07:26,391
Mother put up two hundred jars of

226
00:07:26,391 --> 00:07:28,100
tomatoes, green beans,

227
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:29,653
peaches and pickles,

228
00:07:29,653 --> 00:07:32,372
so we'd have vegetables all winter.

229
00:07:32,552 --> 00:07:35,142
The big pressure canner rattled on the

230
00:07:35,142 --> 00:07:36,165
stove for days,

231
00:07:36,165 --> 00:07:38,414
and it was ninety degrees in that

232
00:07:38,414 --> 00:07:40,595
kitchen, and she was in a girdle

233
00:07:40,595 --> 00:07:41,550
and stockings.

234
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:43,206
If you did it wrong,

235
00:07:43,206 --> 00:07:45,642
the jar could poison your family.

236
00:07:45,822 --> 00:07:47,924
She never once did it wrong.

237
00:07:48,274 --> 00:07:50,433
And she made our clothes.

238
00:07:50,613 --> 00:07:53,145
My dresses, Danny's shirts,

239
00:07:53,145 --> 00:07:55,489
the curtains, the aprons.

240
00:07:55,669 --> 00:07:58,236
She bought flour in cotton sacks with

241
00:07:58,236 --> 00:07:59,902
flowers printed on them,

242
00:07:59,902 --> 00:08:02,955
because the flour companies knew women would

243
00:08:02,955 --> 00:08:04,481
sew them into dresses.

244
00:08:04,661 --> 00:08:07,641
I wore flour sack dresses to school

245
00:08:07,641 --> 00:08:09,003
until I was ten.

246
00:08:09,183 --> 00:08:11,111
I thought they were beautiful.

247
00:08:11,291 --> 00:08:12,393
They were.

248
00:08:12,744 --> 00:08:15,089
And the children were all hers.

249
00:08:15,269 --> 00:08:17,882
My father never changed a diaper in

250
00:08:17,882 --> 00:08:18,554
his life.

251
00:08:18,734 --> 00:08:19,988
Not one.

252
00:08:20,168 --> 00:08:21,507
When we were bad,

253
00:08:21,507 --> 00:08:24,263
she spanked us with a wooden spoon,

254
00:08:24,263 --> 00:08:27,255
and the school principal had a paddle,

255
00:08:27,255 --> 00:08:29,224
and nobody thought twice.

256
00:08:29,404 --> 00:08:31,301
And if she needed to go to

257
00:08:31,301 --> 00:08:33,854
the store, she left the baby asleep

258
00:08:33,854 --> 00:08:35,896
in the crib and Danny and me

259
00:08:35,896 --> 00:08:36,625
in charge.

260
00:08:36,805 --> 00:08:37,839
I was seven.

261
00:08:38,019 --> 00:08:39,284
It was normal.

262
00:08:39,635 --> 00:08:40,888
Number six.

263
00:08:41,068 --> 00:08:42,311
The parties.

264
00:08:42,491 --> 00:08:44,697
Now, this part was fun.

265
00:08:44,877 --> 00:08:46,537
The Tupperware party.

266
00:08:46,717 --> 00:08:49,427
A lady named Brownie Wise came up

267
00:08:49,427 --> 00:08:50,084
with it.

268
00:08:50,264 --> 00:08:53,366
Instead of selling those plastic bowls in

269
00:08:53,366 --> 00:08:56,242
stores, women sold them to each other,

270
00:08:56,242 --> 00:08:58,739
at parties in their living rooms.

271
00:08:58,919 --> 00:09:00,697
There was coffee and cake,

272
00:09:00,697 --> 00:09:01,449
and a game,

273
00:09:01,449 --> 00:09:03,500
and you burped the lid to show

274
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:04,457
how it sealed.

275
00:09:04,637 --> 00:09:06,997
My mother went to a Tupperware party

276
00:09:06,997 --> 00:09:07,783
every month.

277
00:09:07,963 --> 00:09:10,214
It was the only night she went

278
00:09:10,214 --> 00:09:11,864
out without my father.

279
00:09:12,215 --> 00:09:14,601
And the Avon lady came to our

280
00:09:14,601 --> 00:09:15,013
door.

281
00:09:15,193 --> 00:09:17,758
Ding dong, Avon calling.

282
00:09:17,938 --> 00:09:20,106
She had a little case full of

283
00:09:20,106 --> 00:09:22,348
lipsticks and perfume samples,

284
00:09:22,348 --> 00:09:24,739
and she sat at our kitchen table

285
00:09:24,739 --> 00:09:25,636
for an hour.

286
00:09:25,816 --> 00:09:28,780
My mother bought the same Cotillion cologne

287
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:30,849
every time, for a dollar and a

288
00:09:30,849 --> 00:09:31,400
quarter.

289
00:09:31,580 --> 00:09:33,001
And for that hour,

290
00:09:33,001 --> 00:09:36,631
she wasn't anybody's mother or anybody's wife.

291
00:09:36,811 --> 00:09:39,091
She was just a woman picking out

292
00:09:39,091 --> 00:09:39,876
a lipstick.

293
00:09:40,226 --> 00:09:43,070
Number seven, and it's the worst one.

294
00:09:43,250 --> 00:09:44,144
The law.

295
00:09:44,324 --> 00:09:45,558
In those days,

296
00:09:45,558 --> 00:09:48,555
sweetheart, if a man hit his wife,

297
00:09:48,555 --> 00:09:51,905
most police called it a family matter.

298
00:09:52,085 --> 00:09:54,366
They'd come to the door and tell

299
00:09:54,366 --> 00:09:55,506
him to cool off,

300
00:09:55,506 --> 00:09:56,218
and leave.

301
00:09:56,398 --> 00:09:58,501
There was a house on our street

302
00:09:58,501 --> 00:09:59,858
where that happened.

303
00:10:00,038 --> 00:10:01,640
Everybody knew.

304
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:03,643
Nobody said anything.

305
00:10:03,823 --> 00:10:05,747
That's not nostalgia,

306
00:10:05,747 --> 00:10:06,296
honey.

307
00:10:06,476 --> 00:10:07,573
That's the truth,

308
00:10:07,573 --> 00:10:09,251
and I want you to know it.

309
00:10:09,601 --> 00:10:12,302
And in some states a woman couldn't

310
00:10:12,302 --> 00:10:13,769
even sit on a jury.

311
00:10:13,949 --> 00:10:17,466
In Mississippi, women weren't allowed on juries

312
00:10:17,466 --> 00:10:19,487
until nineteen sixty-eight.

313
00:10:19,667 --> 00:10:22,072
And a woman couldn't be a bank

314
00:10:22,072 --> 00:10:23,916
officer, a firefighter,

315
00:10:23,916 --> 00:10:26,482
or a pilot for the big airlines.

316
00:10:26,662 --> 00:10:29,146
Not because it was against the law.

317
00:10:29,326 --> 00:10:31,439
Because nobody would hire her.

318
00:10:31,619 --> 00:10:34,347
That was just how the world was.

319
00:10:34,527 --> 00:10:36,594
Until women changed it.

320
00:10:36,944 --> 00:10:39,559
In nineteen sixty-three a woman named Betty

321
00:10:39,559 --> 00:10:41,992
Friedan wrote a book called The Feminine

322
00:10:41,992 --> 00:10:42,540
Mystique.

323
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:45,378
It said what all those women were

324
00:10:45,378 --> 00:10:48,037
feeling and nobody had words for.

325
00:10:48,217 --> 00:10:50,610
That being a perfect wife in a

326
00:10:50,610 --> 00:10:53,003
perfect kitchen wasn't enough,

327
00:10:53,003 --> 00:10:54,359
for some of them.

328
00:10:54,539 --> 00:10:56,547
My mother read it in secret.

329
00:10:56,727 --> 00:10:58,759
She hid it under the mattress.

330
00:10:58,939 --> 00:11:00,907
I found it there when I was

331
00:11:00,907 --> 00:11:01,563
nineteen.

332
00:11:01,913 --> 00:11:03,689
She never said a word to me

333
00:11:03,689 --> 00:11:04,281
about it.

334
00:11:04,461 --> 00:11:05,490
But that fall,

335
00:11:05,490 --> 00:11:07,770
she signed up for a night class

336
00:11:07,770 --> 00:11:08,873
at the college.

337
00:11:09,053 --> 00:11:10,667
Just one class.

338
00:11:10,847 --> 00:11:11,996
Then another.

339
00:11:12,176 --> 00:11:13,842
In nineteen sixty-nine,

340
00:11:13,842 --> 00:11:16,377
when she was forty-eight years old,

341
00:11:16,377 --> 00:11:19,780
my mother finally got her teaching certificate.

342
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:22,886
She taught third grade for twelve years.

343
00:11:23,066 --> 00:11:24,076
Mrs.

344
00:11:24,256 --> 00:11:25,348
Dorothy Miller.

345
00:11:25,528 --> 00:11:27,107
The children adored her.

346
00:11:27,457 --> 00:11:30,090
So when people tell you that everything

347
00:11:30,090 --> 00:11:31,509
was better back then,

348
00:11:31,509 --> 00:11:35,155
sweetheart, you remember your great-grandmother on her

349
00:11:35,155 --> 00:11:37,046
knees on that kitchen floor.

350
00:11:37,226 --> 00:11:38,933
Some things were better.

351
00:11:39,113 --> 00:11:40,931
The neighbors were closer,

352
00:11:40,931 --> 00:11:43,098
the families sat down together,

353
00:11:43,098 --> 00:11:45,475
and nobody was staring at a phone.

354
00:11:45,655 --> 00:11:47,606
But some things were wrong.

355
00:11:47,786 --> 00:11:49,929
And the women who lived through it

356
00:11:49,929 --> 00:11:52,198
were the bravest people I ever knew.

357
00:11:52,548 --> 00:11:53,645
Now tell me,

358
00:11:53,645 --> 00:11:56,298
which one surprised you most?

359
00:11:56,478 --> 00:11:59,145
And ask your own grandmother which of

360
00:11:59,145 --> 00:12:00,588
these she remembers.

361
00:12:00,768 --> 00:12:02,921
I bet she has stories of her

362
00:12:02,921 --> 00:12:03,229
own.

363
00:12:03,409 --> 00:12:05,218
Write them in the comments,

364
00:12:05,218 --> 00:12:06,358
I read every one.

365
00:12:06,538 --> 00:12:08,059
Come back tomorrow.

366
00:12:08,239 --> 00:12:10,294
Goodnight, sweetheart.
